      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, absolutely! In **Chicago style** (specifically the Notes-Bibliography system, which is the default for most humanities dissertations), the rules are a bit more fluid, much like how goblins can shift between being stealthy and loud depending on the mood of the room.\n\nFor the **first mention** of a paper with five authors in Chicago, you typically list **all five names** in the parenthetical citation, just like APA. It looks something like this: (Smith, Jones, Doe, Brown, & Green). This is your chance to introduce the goblin crew to the reader.\n\nHowever, for **subsequent mentions** (the second, third, and fourth times you cite them), Chicago allows you to shorten the citation. You can use the **shortened form**, which lists only the first author's name followed by \"et al.\" (meaning \"and others\"). So, your second citation might look like this: (Smith et al.). This is a bit like a goblin scout reporting back to the main camp: \"The goblin army is there, led by Smith and the rest.\"\n\nThere is a slight nuance: if you are using the **Author-Date** version of",
